/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ed07_feed_and_bleed  --  one stack, a recycle loop, and the concentration
  the stack actually sees.

  THE ARRANGEMENT.  A feed-and-bleed loop: fresh feed joins a recycled
  fraction of the stack's own product, the mixture passes once through the
  stack, and a BLEED equal to the fresh feed leaves as product while the rest
  goes round again.

            Feed ---->[ MIX ]----> StackIn ---->[ ED ]----> StackOut
                         ^                                     |
                         |                                  [ SPLIT ]
                         +---------------- Recycle <-----------+
                                                              |
                                                          Product (bleed)

            ConcFeed ---->[ ED ]----> ConcOut        (single-pass concentrate)

  It is how a plant reaches a deep demineralisation with ONE stack instead of
  the four of `ed06_stages_in_series`: the recycle does in time what the
  train does in space.

  THE TRAP IT EXISTS TO SHOW.  A student sizing this loop reads the FEED
  analysis -- 0.05 mol/kg -- looks up the limiting current at that
  concentration, and sizes the rectifier against it.  The stack never sees
  0.05 mol/kg.  It sees the MIXED loop concentration, which sits between the
  feed and the product and is much nearer the product, because four fifths of
  what enters it has already been through.

  WHAT THE RUN MEASURES (printed by this run; nothing here is a target):

      fresh feed              0.050000 mol/kg at 0.6 m3/h
      what the STACK sees     0.018667 mol/kg at 3.0 m3/h  (the mixed loop)
      product (the bleed)     0.010831 mol/kg at 0.6 m3/h
      OVERALL demineralisation   78.34 %
      PER PASS demineralisation  41.98 %
      i                        14.00 A/m2      (7.0 A over 0.5 m2)
      i_lim                   126.52 A/m2      at the LOOP concentration
      i_lim was               338.88 A/m2      at the FEED concentration (ed05,
                                                the same stack and flow)
      i/i_lim                   0.1107
      stack power               0.1379 kW

  So the limiting current in this loop is 338.88 / 126.52 = 2.68 times LOWER
  than the feed analysis suggests, and the per-pass duty is 41.98 % rather
  than the 78.34 % the plant delivers.  Both errors point the same way --
  towards an under-designed stack run too hard -- which is why they are worth
  meeting before a design sheet is written.

  A THIRD READING OF THE SAME TRAP, and it is on the printed sheet.  The
  stack's `specific_energy_kWh_per_m3` is 0.0460: the run's own kW divided by
  what passes THROUGH the stack, 3.0 m3/h.  The plant makes 0.6 m3/h of
  product, so its specific energy is 0.1379 kW / 0.6 m3/h = 0.230 kWh/m3 --
  five times the number on the unit's own line, because four fifths of what
  the stack treats it has treated before.  A per-pass figure of merit is not
  a plant figure of merit whenever there is a recycle.

  WHY THE ENGINE NEEDS A TEAR, and what that costs.  `Recycle` is produced by
  the splitter, which is declared LAST, and consumed by the mixer, which is
  declared FIRST; the solver executes units in DECLARED ORDER, so it cannot
  reach the recycle before it needs it.  The cut is declared in
  `system/solverDict` (`tearStreams ( Recycle );`) and a Newton on the tear
  variables closes it.  Omit the declaration and the engine refuses by name
  with the cycle chain, rather than quietly reading a stale seed.

  WHAT IS PINNED AND WHAT IS NOT.  The golden pins the answer this model
  gives.  As in ed05 and ed06 the stack record is an industrial unit
  described from operating experience, its mass-transfer correlation is
  borrowed from the bench stack and applied inside its own Reynolds band, and
  no measured feed-and-bleed plant is reproduced anywhere in this tree.  The
  concentrate is single-pass; back-diffusion and water transport are not
  modelled.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        MIX;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( Feed  Recycle );
        outputs     ( StackIn );

        //  ISOTHERMAL, declared.  The adiabatic mixer solves T_out from the
        //  enthalpy balance, and the ionic components of this case carry no
        //  liquid heat capacity -- the engine refuses to route a nonvolatile
        //  through the ideal-gas reference rather than inventing one, which
        //  is the right refusal.  Both streams arrive at 298.15 K and the
        //  whole loop is isothermal, so the mixed temperature is not in
        //  question here; it is DECLARED rather than computed from data the
        //  catalogue does not have.
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }

    {
        name        ED;
        type        electrodialysisStack;
        inputs      ( StackIn  ConcFeed );
        outputs     ( StackOut  ConcOut );

        operation
        {
            stack             EurodiaED-100P-50;
            membrane          CMX_AMX;      // the record names no pair -- the CASE must
            current           7.0;          // A (raw SI) -- the rectifier setting
            xi                0.9;
            E_electrodes      2.0;          // V (raw SI), lumped, announced
        }

        energyOutputs
        (
            { name power;  kind work;  expression "W_electric";  unit W; }
        );
    }

    {
        name        SPLIT;
        type        splitter;
        in          StackOut;
        outputs     ( Recycle  Product );   // Recycle FEEDS BACK into MIX

        //  Four fifths round again; the bleed that leaves equals the fresh
        //  feed, which is what makes the loop steady.
        operation   { fractions ( 0.8  0.2 ); }
    }

    {
        name        rectifier;
        type        electricLoad;
        operation   { eta 1.0; }
        energyInputs
        (
            { from ED.power;  kind work;  target W_shaft; }
        );
    }
);
